---
import SiteLayout from "../../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../../../../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
const sections = [
  {title:"Access the Cisco IOS command-line interface",ref:"IMG-0103",text:"Cisco IOS separates command-line access into modes. User EXEC mode is the limited starting mode, shown by the > prompt. Privileged EXEC mode is shown by # and provides access to monitoring and administrative commands. Use enable to enter privileged EXEC mode. Use configure terminal to enter global configuration mode, where device-wide settings are changed."},
  {title:"Navigate command modes and syntax",ref:"IMG-0109",text:"The prompt identifies the current mode. Global configuration mode uses (config)#. Interface configuration uses (config-if)#, and line configuration uses (config-line)#. A command consists of a command name plus any required keywords and arguments. Context-sensitive help uses ? to show available commands, keywords, and arguments for the current mode. For example, enter interface ? at Switch(config)# to list valid interface types in that context. Tab completes a command prefix only when it uniquely identifies the command; if the prefix is ambiguous, use ? to inspect the available choices. When output pauses at --More--, Space advances a page and Enter advances a line."},
  {title:"Apply a basic device configuration",ref:"IMG-0117",text:"A basic switch or router setup commonly includes a meaningful hostname, a protected privileged EXEC password using enable secret, console and remote-line settings, and a login banner. Configure console access under line console 0. Configure remote terminal access under line vty. The login command requires a line password; login local uses the local username database. service password-encryption obscures supported plaintext line passwords in the configuration, but it is not a replacement for strong secrets or secure management protocols."},
  {title:"Understand running and startup configuration",ref:"IMG-0136",text:"The running-config is the active configuration held in RAM. Changes affect the device immediately but can be lost after a restart if they are not saved. The startup-config is stored in NVRAM and is loaded during boot. Save the active configuration with copy running-config startup-config. Verify saved and active settings with show startup-config and show running-config."},
  {title:"Configure management addressing",ref:"IMG-0116",text:"An IPv4 address is represented in dotted-decimal notation as four decimal octets, each from 0 through 255. A 32-bit subnet mask identifies the network and host portions of the address. A default gateway is the router address a host uses to reach destinations outside its local subnet. A Layer 2 switch can be assigned a management IP address on an SVI such as interface vlan 1, with no shutdown, and an ip default-gateway for remote management."},
  {title:"Verify connectivity and interface state",ref:"IMG-0120",text:"Use show ip interface brief to inspect interface names, assigned IP addresses, administrative/operational status, and line protocol state. Use ping to test reachability and traceroute (tracert on Windows) to examine the path toward a destination. A successful interface configuration does not by itself prove end-to-end connectivity; check addressing, masks, gateway settings, link state, and the remote path."}
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Module 2: Basic Switch and End Device Configuration — fred.fish" description="A source-traceable Cisco IOS lesson covering access modes, command syntax, configuration, addressing, and verification." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 02" label="MODULE 2 / CISCO IOS FUNDAMENTALS" /><h1>Configure the device.<br /><span>Verify what changed.</span></h1><p>Learn the IOS command hierarchy, configure a switch or router safely, save changes, and verify management connectivity.</p><div class="lesson-meta"><span>6 LESSON SECTIONS</span><span>SOURCE IMAGES REFERENCED</span></div></header>
    <div class="lesson-callout"><strong>Core workflow</strong><span>Access the CLI → enter the correct mode → configure → save → verify.</span></div>
    <section class="lesson-sections">{sections.map((section,index)=><article class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">{String(index+1).padStart(2,"0")}</div><div><h2>{section.title}</h2><p>{section.text}</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>{section.ref}</code></div></div></article>)}</section>
    <section class="command-reference"><Eyebrow label="COMMAND QUICK REFERENCE" /><h2>Common IOS commands</h2><div class="command-grid">
      <div><code>enable</code><span>Enter privileged EXEC mode.</span></div><div><code>configure terminal</code><span>Enter global configuration mode.</span></div><div><code>interface ?</code><span>Show valid interface types in the current configuration context.</span></div><div><code>Tab</code><span>Complete a command prefix when it uniquely identifies a command.</span></div><div><code>show running-config</code><span>Display the active configuration.</span></div><div><code>copy running-config startup-config</code><span>Save active settings to startup configuration.</span></div><div><code>show ip interface brief</code><span>Summarize interface addresses and status.</span></div><div><code>service password-encryption</code><span>Obscure supported plaintext passwords in configuration output.</span></div>
    </div></section>
    <section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Apply the commands in practice.</h2><p>Practice questions target IOS modes, configuration persistence, addressing, and verification.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
